#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187987 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e1b67a-cd41-5edc-90b9-941007bfc39c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/id #uuid "2f27b473-e10a-5e77-8bb2-d8ae5d0ecf4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10037" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=9$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $50$ and the sample standard deviation is $1.5$, find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 3.355) = 0.005$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma^2$.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution $T_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n- $S^2$ is the sample variance, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{n-1,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=50, \\qquad n = 9, \\qquad s = 1.5\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.99=0.01 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.005\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(T > 3.355) = 0.005\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nt_{n-1,\\alpha/2} = t_{8,0.005} = 3.355\n$$\n\n![](images/84/84f060ec1a5e8a8b3db0c0866ebef9db1c242e944717a5abfd28447e0eddb9be.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}50 & ± 3.355 \\cdot \\frac{1.5}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\ 50 & ± 1.678\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187988 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "778f1426-e8a0-597f-84f6-337359a553c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/id #uuid "c2e2bcbc-ab4b-5a0a-a803-cb98326017ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-10040" :question/problem "A tire manufacturer measures the stopping distance of a new tire model from $60 \\, \\textrm{mph}$ on a standard test car. A sample of $81$ stopping distances yielded a sample mean of $132\\, \\textrm{ft}$ with a sample standard deviation of $5.4 \\, \\textrm{ft}$. Find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the mean stopping distance $\\mu$ of these tires.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k,p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$.*\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $79$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.374\\,$ | $\\,2.640\\,$ |\n| $80$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.374\\,$ | $\\,2.639\\,$ |\n| $81$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.373\\,$ | $\\,2.638\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance $\\sigma^2$. However, the sample size\n\n$$\nn = 81 \\geq 30\n$$\n\nis *sufficiently large*.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution $T_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n- $S^2$ is the sample variance, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{n-1,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=132 \\, \\textrm{ft}, \\qquad n = 81, \\qquad s = 5.4 \\, \\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value $t_{80,0.025}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{80, 0.025}) = 0.025\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=81-1=80$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/d5/d513fbeee39a42a5e897c1b171d7e820196066517c5aa1c6c12549a98ce00ddd.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that\n\n$$\nt_{80,0.025}=1.990{:}\n$$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.025}$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $79$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.374$ | $2.640$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}80}$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.990}$ | $2.374$ | $2.639$ |\n| $81$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.373$ | $2.638$ |\n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}132 & ± 1.99 \\cdot \\frac{5.4}{\\sqrt{81}}ft \\\\ 132 & ± 1.194ft\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187989 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81c8b2bc-d215-5bae-b81f-05870dd39163" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/id #uuid "7446f45b-6d41-5a11-8006-853b38fa5142" :question/math-academy-id "e-10047" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=100$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $30$, and the sample standard deviation is $8$, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.66) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $99$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance $\\sigma^2$. However, the sample size\n\n$$\nn = 100 \\geq 30\n$$\n\nis *sufficiently large*.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution $T_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n- $S^2$ is the sample variance, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{n-1,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=30, \\qquad n = 100, \\qquad s = 8\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(T > 1.66) = 0.05\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nt_{n-1,\\alpha/2} = t_{99,0.05} = 1.66\n$$\n\n![](images/8d/8d4cd3a76fffee915059c849d3bac4ca395deb3e2cfe5ab1701e9ee4737b4401.png)\n\nTherefore, the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}30 & ± 1.66 \\cdot \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\ 30 & ± 1.328\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "778f1426-e8a0-597f-84f6-337359a553c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b30508ee-1eea-506b-838b-162d9f27832b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120 ± 3.925g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185605} {:answer/id #uuid "c9eef0a1-2188-5c8a-9b26-ca5a37ba7d84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18 ± 3.925g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185606} {:answer/id #uuid "218fe599-cbd3-5cc0-831a-d71fff91f213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120 ± 1.962g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185607} {:answer/id #uuid "31572c11-b5e5-529c-86a0-7435dc62b4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120 ± 5.278g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185608} {:answer/id #uuid "7daf4a1a-0cf6-515b-ac4f-96dc17fdaf20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18 ± 7.152g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185609}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7a298a57-d38a-51dd-8fbd-ddef7664c294" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185604}] :question/id #uuid "3a4751d0-f8ad-5ca6-9ca9-6da91f8c5dab" :question/math-academy-id "q-142395" :question/problem "A farmer grows a particular variety of apples. He sampled $81$ apples from the harvest and found that the sample mean weight of apples is $120g$ and the sample standard deviation is $18g$. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean weight $μ$ for the entire harvest.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k, p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k, p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$.*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $79$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.374$ | $2.640$ |\n| $80$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.374$ | $2.639$ |\n| $81$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.373$ | $2.638$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e1b67a-cd41-5edc-90b9-941007bfc39c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be0fedc3-54cf-5da0-be63-7eb94be111a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42 ± 6.71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185611} {:answer/id #uuid "ef90143b-1a72-537d-90f9-c5a893fabd1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42 ± 7.196" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185612} {:answer/id #uuid "11cc788c-ed34-596c-bf30-4f842068f778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42 ± 3.212" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185613} {:answer/id #uuid "2b15c2d5-3972-5f43-bba6-afcec36436c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "99 ± 7.196" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185614} {:answer/id #uuid "631173df-4d35-5b88-95a5-8376e0618ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "99 ± 3.212" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185615}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4e8d330b-a65f-526a-88ee-75aa78b19a07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185610}] :question/id #uuid "dc8102d8-eaf2-5af9-98b6-20a38a45681f" :question/math-academy-id "q-142448" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n = 9$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $42$ and the sample standard deviation is $6$, find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $μ$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 3.355) = 0.005$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e1b67a-cd41-5edc-90b9-941007bfc39c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "842471a3-25be-50cb-b1b8-88da4fa4e4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50 ± 1.753" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185617} {:answer/id #uuid "d36e02c5-85cf-5bc2-b38b-331567de33ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 ± 3.506" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185618} {:answer/id #uuid "f878c926-29a0-5575-ad8a-34d0c71c0bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50 ± 2.415" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185619} {:answer/id #uuid "3f729211-65cb-507b-8cdd-8b3a281a30a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 ± 2.415" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185620} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff45909-16a2-5588-9eab-48aa27ed7e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50 ± 3.506" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185621}] :answer-field/id #uuid "213abdf6-e2d7-5205-ae3f-9d57185fc89e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185616}] :question/id #uuid "9109bebb-ab30-51e2-97b6-5e0e69a9d363" :question/math-academy-id "q-142449" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b5037831d90f93fb6b8b0603655162e4f362a638b35c569f7c5e07007974b3d.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n = 16$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $50$, and the sample standard deviation is $8$, find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $μ$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.753) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $15$ degrees of freedom, as shown in the diagram above.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
